    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d194">

                <pb facs="0238" n="1072" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1072"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 103.96/1245</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-10-22T17:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 22, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5976. For McFadden [from
                    War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 394. Your 476, Embassy’s 5306, October 4, 12 noon,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d194-f1"/> and your 443, Embassy 5175, September 23, 11 p.m.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d194-f2"/> We approve in substance of the plan of September 7
                    accompanying your letter of September 11 for black list for Switzerland, as
                    modified to meet objections of French.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d194-f3"/> We do
                    not, however, favor the presentation of a joint note to the Swiss on this
                    subject, believing that representations with respect to the black list should be
                    kept informal. The substance of the note should therefore, in so far as United
                    States is concerned, be orally presented. Of course, it is to be kept clear, as
                    matter of record, that the War Trade Board bases its black list upon the
                    recommendations of its own representative and is not bound by the decision of
                    the Paris or Berne committees.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
